{"title":"SDLC & Agile Methodologies","summary":"The Software Development Life Cycle (SDLC) describes the phases a software project moves through: requirements gathering, design, implementation, testing, deployment, and maintenance — every methodology organizes these same phases differently, not invents entirely different activities. The Waterfall model runs these phases sequentially and mostly one-directionally, completing each phase (with sign-off) before starting the next, giving predictable milestones and thorough documentation but struggling badly when requirements are uncertain or change mid-project, since a problem discovered during testing means revisiting a phase completed months earlier. Agile methodologies instead structure development as short, iterative cycles each producing a working increment, explicitly valuing (per the Agile Manifesto) individuals and interactions over processes and tools, working software over comprehensive documentation, customer collaboration over contract negotiation, and responding to change over following a rigid plan — this doesn't mean documentation or planning don't matter, just that Agile prioritizes adaptability when the two are in tension. Scrum, the most widely used Agile framework, organizes work into fixed-length sprints (commonly 1-4 weeks), with defined roles (Product Owner prioritizing what gets built, Scrum Master facilitating and removing blockers, Development Team building it) and recurring ceremonies (sprint planning, a daily stand-up, a sprint review, and a sprint retrospective). Neither approach is universally correct: Waterfall's upfront clarity suits projects with genuinely stable requirements (certain regulated or safety-critical systems), while Agile's adaptability suits projects where requirements are expected to evolve as stakeholders see working software.","learning_outcomes":["List the standard SDLC phases and explain how Waterfall and Agile organize the same phases differently.","Explain the four Agile Manifesto value statements and what each means in practice for prioritizing work.","Describe the purpose of each standard Scrum ceremony and each standard role."],"common_mistakes":["Assuming Agile means 'no planning' or 'no documentation' — the Manifesto values adaptability more than rigid plans in cases of tension, not their complete absence.","Assuming Waterfall is simply 'the old, wrong way' — its upfront clarity genuinely suits projects with stable, well-understood requirements, like certain safety-critical systems.","Confusing the Scrum Master with a traditional project manager who assigns tasks — the Scrum Master facilitates and removes blockers, while the Development Team is self-organizing.","Treating a daily stand-up as a detailed status meeting — it's meant to be a brief synchronization, not an extended discussion or problem-solving session."],"worked_example":{"setup":"A team is building a login feature. Partway through implementation, a stakeholder realizes an additional requirement (two-factor authentication) is needed. Trace how this is handled under Waterfall versus Scrum.","steps":["Under Waterfall: requirements and design were already signed off, so the new requirement means formally reopening the requirements phase, revising sign-off, redoing the affected design, then resuming implementation.","Under Scrum: the new requirement is added to the product backlog, prioritized by the Product Owner relative to everything else not yet started.","Under Scrum, the current sprint's committed work continues undisturbed, and the new item is planned into a future sprint during that sprint's planning session.","Under Scrum, the original login feature ships in its planned sprint, and two-factor authentication ships as a separate, later increment."],"result":"Waterfall forces the change to be resolved by formally reopening completed phases before implementation can proceed, while Scrum absorbs it by adding a backlog item and scheduling it into a future sprint without disrupting already-committed work."}}
{"title":"Requirements Engineering & Design Patterns","summary":"Requirements engineering is the process of discovering, documenting, and validating what a software system must actually do — functional requirements specify what the system does (a specific behavior, like 'the system shall allow a user to reset their password'), while non-functional requirements specify how well it does it (qualities like performance, security, or availability, such as 'the system shall respond to 95% of requests within 200ms'), and both matter, since a functionally complete system that's too slow or insecure has still failed its purpose. Requirements are typically elicited through stakeholder interviews, surveys, observing users' workflows, and analyzing existing systems, then captured as use cases (a specific actor-system interaction, step by step, including alternate and error paths) or user stories ('As a [role], I want [goal], so that [benefit]', favored in Agile for brevity) — both aim to make an abstract need concrete enough to design and test against. A Software Requirements Specification (SRS), when used, consolidates these into a single reference, and traceability (linking each requirement to the design, code, and tests that implement and verify it) lets a team confirm every requirement was addressed and understand the impact of a change. Requirements directly shape design, especially non-functional ones: a high-availability requirement pushes toward redundancy and failover, a strict privacy requirement pushes toward encryption and access control, and a massive-concurrent-load requirement pushes toward horizontally-scalable architecture — design is substantially constrained and directed by requirements, not a purely creative exercise disconnected from them. Because vaguely gathered or late-validated requirements are one of the most common root causes of project failure, disciplined requirements engineering is foundational to every later SDLC phase succeeding.","learning_outcomes":["Given a system description, classify statements as functional or non-functional requirements, and identify which quality attribute each non-functional requirement addresses.","Write a user story in standard format for a given feature, and expand it into a more detailed use case including at least one alternate or error path.","Given a non-functional requirement, explain a concrete design decision it would drive, and explain what traceability means in connecting it to the resulting design."],"common_mistakes":["Treating non-functional requirements as less important than functional ones — a system meeting every functional requirement but failing a critical non-functional one (too slow, insecure) has still failed the actual need.","Writing a use case or user story so vague it can't be designed or tested against — a requirement needs to be concrete enough that a team can unambiguously determine whether it's satisfied.","Gathering requirements once at project start and never revisiting them — elicitation and validation are often iterative, especially as stakeholders see early working software.","Confusing 'design patterns' in the requirements-to-design translation sense with the specific catalog of Gang of Four patterns — the latter is a narrower, separate topic."],"worked_example":{"setup":"A hospital wants a system for nurses to record patient vital signs. Identify a functional requirement, a non-functional requirement, write a user story, and identify a design implication.","steps":["Functional requirement: 'The system shall allow a nurse to record blood pressure, heart rate, and temperature, timestamped automatically.'","Non-functional requirement: 'The system shall remain available and responsive even during a network outage in the ward' (availability, since an unusable system during an outage could directly harm patient care).","User story: 'As a nurse, I want to record a patient's vital signs quickly at the bedside, so that I can spend more time on direct patient care.'","Design implication: the availability requirement pushes toward local, offline-capable data entry (caching entries on a bedside device, syncing once connectivity returns) rather than requiring a live connection for every entry."],"result":"The functional requirement specifies what data must be captured, the non-functional requirement specifies an availability quality that isn't optional given the stakes, and it concretely drives the design toward offline-capable, locally-cached entry — showing a non-functional requirement shaping architecture, not just a checklist note."}}
{"title":"Testing & Quality Assurance","summary":"Testing verifies software behaves correctly, organized into levels testing progressively larger pieces: unit tests verify a single function or class in isolation (dependencies often replaced by mocks/stubs), integration tests verify multiple units work correctly together (catching interface mismatches unit tests can't see), system tests verify the complete assembled application against its requirements, and acceptance tests verify the system meets actual business/user needs, often with stakeholders directly. Testing is also categorized by internal knowledge used: black-box testing designs cases purely from the specification without looking at the implementation, while white-box testing uses knowledge of internal code structure to exercise particular paths — both catch different bug classes, since black-box catches specification violations while white-box catches implementation-specific edge cases. Effective test case design uses equivalence partitioning (grouping inputs into classes expected to be handled the same way, testing one representative per class) and boundary value analysis (testing at and just around the edges of valid ranges, since off-by-one errors are disproportionately common at boundaries). Test-Driven Development (TDD) inverts the usual order by writing a failing test before the code that makes it pass, then refactoring with that test as a safety net — proponents argue this produces more thoroughly tested, better-designed code by forcing a clear specification before implementation. Code coverage measures what percentage of code was executed by the test suite, a useful signal for finding untested code but not itself a measure of correctness — 100% coverage means every line ran at least once, not that every line was tested with every input that could reveal a bug.","learning_outcomes":["Given a software component, identify what would be tested at the unit, integration, system, and acceptance levels respectively.","Given a function's input specification, apply equivalence partitioning and boundary value analysis to design a minimal but effective set of test cases.","Explain why 100% code coverage doesn't guarantee correctness, with a concrete example of a fully-covered line that could still contain an undetected bug."],"common_mistakes":["Assuming unit tests alone are sufficient — they verify components in isolation and can miss bugs that only appear when components interact, which integration tests are specifically designed to catch.","Testing only 'normal' expected inputs and skipping boundary values — many real bugs occur disproportionately at boundaries, exactly why boundary value analysis is a deliberate technique.","Treating code coverage percentage as a direct measure of test quality — a line can be executed by a test that doesn't actually check its output is correct, so high coverage with weak assertions can still hide bugs.","Assuming TDD means writing all tests before any code — it's typically practiced incrementally: one small failing test, then just enough code to pass it, then refactoring, in a tight cycle."],"worked_example":{"setup":"A function accepts an integer 'age' valid for 0 to 120 inclusive, rejecting anything outside. Design a minimal, effective test case set using equivalence partitioning and boundary value analysis.","steps":["Identify equivalence classes: valid (0-120, accepted) and two invalid classes (below 0, above 120, rejected) — three classes, needing at least one representative each.","Apply boundary value analysis to the valid range's edges: minimum (0), maximum (120), and values just outside each boundary (-1, 121).","Combine into a compact set: {-5, -1, 0, 60, 120, 121, 150} covering well-below-range, boundary-below, min-boundary, mid-valid, max-boundary, boundary-above, and well-above-range.","This 7-value set covers all three equivalence classes and every boundary, rather than testing dozens of arbitrary valid-range values that would mostly just re-confirm the same 'accept' behavior."],"result":"A carefully chosen 7-value set systematically covers every equivalence class and boundary — catching the bugs most likely to actually exist (at boundaries, across distinct behavior classes) with a small, deliberate, non-redundant test set."}}
{"title":"Version Control (Git)","summary":"Git is a distributed version control system that tracks a project's history as a sequence of commits, each a complete snapshot of tracked files at that point (not just a diff, though Git internally stores this efficiently), linked to its parent commit(s) to form a history graph — it's 'distributed' specifically because every clone contains the full history, not just a pointer to a central server's copy, allowing offline work and multiple independent remotes. Git's working model has three areas: the working directory (files being edited), the staging area/index (where specific changes are explicitly added via 'git add' for the next commit), and the repository (the committed history) — this staging step lets a commit be deliberately curated to include exactly the intended changes. A branch is simply a lightweight, movable pointer to a specific commit (not a full copy of the codebase), which is what makes branching fast and cheap, and merging combines changes from one branch into another: a fast-forward merge simply moves the target branch's pointer forward when there's a linear path with no divergence, while a true three-way merge (when both branches have diverged) compares the two tips against their common ancestor to automatically combine non-conflicting changes, flagging a conflict only when the same lines were changed differently on both branches. Rebase offers an alternative to merging: instead of a new merge commit joining two histories, rebase replays one branch's commits on top of another branch's tip, producing linear history without a merge commit — this rewrites history (new commit identifiers even for identical content), which is why rebasing already-shared commits is generally discouraged, since it can diverge from what collaborators already have. Understanding this underlying model (commits as snapshots in a graph, branches as pointers, staging as deliberate curation) makes Git's many commands comprehensible as consequences of this structure rather than a list to memorize independently.","learning_outcomes":["Explain the three-area model (working directory, staging area, repository) and trace what happens to a modified file through 'git add' and 'git commit'.","Given two branches with a specified commit history, determine whether merging would be a fast-forward or require a true three-way merge, and identify whether a conflict would occur.","Explain the difference between merge and rebase for integrating changes, including why rebase creates new commit identifiers and why rebasing shared commits is discouraged."],"common_mistakes":["Assuming a Git commit stores only a diff — conceptually, each commit represents the complete state of tracked files, even though internal storage is optimized to avoid literally duplicating unchanged content.","Assuming a branch is a separate copy of the entire codebase — a branch is just a movable pointer to a commit, which is exactly why creating and switching branches is fast and lightweight.","Assuming every merge requires manual conflict resolution — a fast-forward or a three-way merge with no overlapping changed lines both complete automatically; a conflict only arises when both branches modified the same lines differently.","Assuming rebase and merge produce equivalent results with just different history shapes — rebase rewrites commit history, which is exactly why rebasing already-shared commits can cause collaborators' local history to diverge from the rewritten version."],"worked_example":{"setup":"Main has commits A→B→C. A feature branch was created at B and added commit D (A→B→D). Determine whether merging feature into main is a fast-forward or requires a three-way merge.","steps":["Identify the common ancestor of main (A→B→C) and feature (A→B→D): both share commit B as their most recent common point.","Check whether main is a direct ancestor of feature's tip or vice versa: main progressed independently to C, feature progressed independently to D — neither is an ancestor of the other.","Since both branches diverged (each has a commit the other doesn't), fast-forward isn't possible — Git must perform a true three-way merge comparing C and D against common ancestor B.","Whether this completes automatically or conflicts depends on whether C and D modified the same lines — different parts combine automatically, same lines changed differently flag a conflict."],"result":"Merging feature into main requires a true three-way merge (not a fast-forward), because both branches added independent commits since their common ancestor B — a fast-forward would only be possible if main had remained unchanged at B while feature moved ahead."}}
{"title":"Software Architecture Patterns","summary":"Software architecture patterns are reusable, high-level solutions to how a system's major components are organized and communicate — distinct from the class-and-object-level design patterns covered separately, architecture patterns operate at the scale of entire subsystems, services, or applications. Layered (N-tier) architecture organizes a system into horizontal layers (commonly presentation, business logic, data access), where each layer depends only on the layer directly below it, keeping concerns separated and letting a layer be modified with minimal impact on others, though strict layering can add indirection overhead for operations that conceptually span multiple layers. The monolith-versus-microservices choice is central for larger systems: a monolith deploys the entire application as a single unit, simpler to develop and deploy initially but requiring the whole system be redeployed for any change and scaling only as one unit; microservices split the system into independently deployable services communicating over a network, allowing independent scaling and technology choices per service, at the cost of substantially more operational complexity (network calls that can fail, distributed data consistency, coordination overhead). Event-driven architecture structures a system around producing and reacting to events (a service publishes 'order placed' without knowing which other services will react), decoupling producers from consumers and allowing new consumers without modifying the producer, at the cost of making overall behavior harder to trace end-to-end from any single component's code. Model-View-Controller (MVC) separates the Model (data and business logic), the View (what's presented to the user), and the Controller (handles input and coordinates Model/View updates) — this separation is what allows the same Model to be presented through multiple different Views without duplicating business logic, and choosing among these patterns is fundamentally about trading simplicity for flexibility/scalability, which is why architecture often deliberately evolves (starting as a monolith, later splitting into microservices) as scale and team structure change.","learning_outcomes":["Compare layered (N-tier) architecture, microservices, and monolithic architecture in terms of deployment unit, scalability, and operational complexity.","Explain how event-driven architecture decouples producers from consumers, and identify a trade-off this decoupling introduces.","Explain MVC's three components and describe a concrete benefit of separating them, such as supporting multiple Views for one Model."],"common_mistakes":["Assuming microservices are unconditionally better than a monolith — they trade increased operational complexity for independent scaling and deployment, a trade-off not worth it for every system, especially smaller or early-stage ones.","Assuming strict layering means every operation must pass through every layer — layering is about dependency direction and separation of concerns, not a rule that every operation must traverse all layers.","Believing event-driven architecture eliminates the need to understand component interactions — decoupling makes adding consumers easier, but tracing a request's full path now requires crossing multiple independently-deployed, indirectly-connected components.","Confusing MVC's Controller with the application's core business logic — the Controller handles input and coordination; business logic and data belong in the Model."],"worked_example":{"setup":"A user submits a form to update their profile name in an application using MVC. Trace which component handles each step.","steps":["The user submits the form; the Controller receives this input.","The Controller validates it and, if valid, tells the Model to update the stored profile name.","The Model updates its internal data and, in many implementations, notifies that a change occurred.","The View, notified by the Model or re-queried by the Controller, re-renders to display the updated name."],"result":"Each component played a distinct role — Controller handled input and orchestration, Model owned and changed the data, View reflected the change — and because the Model has no direct knowledge of the View, the same update logic would work identically if a completely different View drove the same Controller."}}
{"title":"Classes & Objects","summary":"A class is a blueprint defining a category of objects — specifying what attributes (fields, holding state) and methods (behavior) every object of that class will have — while an object is a specific instance of a class, with its own actual values for those attributes, created via a constructor, a special method that initializes a new object's state. Encapsulation bundles an object's data with the methods that operate on it, restricting direct external access using access modifiers (private, protected, public), so a class's internal representation can be hidden behind a public interface — this lets a class's internal implementation change later without breaking code elsewhere that depends only on the public interface, and lets the class enforce its own invariants, since external code can't bypass a method to corrupt internal state directly. Every object typically has an implicit reference to itself ('this' in Java/C++, 'self' in Python), used within a class's methods to refer to the specific object the method was called on, distinguishing 'this object's data' from a same-named local variable, and letting a method modify or read the calling object's specific state. Two objects of the same class share the same structure (attributes and methods defined by the class) but hold entirely independent state — changing one object's attribute has no effect on another, since each object has its own separate copy of instance data, even though they share the exact same method code defined once on the class. Understanding the class-versus-object distinction is the foundational concept underlying every other object-oriented idea, from inheritance to polymorphism to design patterns.","learning_outcomes":["Given a class definition, distinguish which parts describe every object of that class versus what's unique to a specific object's instance state.","Explain how encapsulation lets a class change its internal implementation without breaking code that depends only on its public interface.","Explain what 'this' (or 'self') refers to within an instance method, and trace how it lets the same method code correctly operate on different objects' independent state."],"common_mistakes":["Confusing a class with an object — a class is a single definition written once, while objects are potentially many independent instances created from it, each with their own state.","Assuming encapsulation is just about hiding data for its own sake — its practical value is enabling internal implementation changes without breaking external dependents, and letting the class enforce its own invariants.","Assuming all objects of the same class share the same attribute values — objects share structure, but each has independent state; modifying one object's field has no effect on another's same-named field.","Forgetting a constructor's job is specifically to initialize a new object's state — treating it like any other method can lead to objects being used before proper initialization."],"worked_example":{"setup":"A class BankAccount has a private field 'balance' and methods deposit(amount) and getBalance(). Two objects, accountA and accountB, both start at balance 0. accountA.deposit(100) is called. Determine each object's balance afterward.","steps":["Both objects are instantiated with their own independent 'balance' field, initialized to 0.","accountA.deposit(100) is called: 'this' refers specifically to accountA, so it modifies accountA's own balance: 0+100=100.","accountB was never involved — its independent balance field remains untouched.","accountA.getBalance() now returns 100; accountB.getBalance() still returns 0."],"result":"accountA's balance is 100 and accountB's is 0 — even though both share the exact same class definition, each holds completely independent state, demonstrating that calling a method on one object never affects another object's separate instance data."}}
{"title":"Inheritance & Polymorphism","summary":"Inheritance lets a class (the subclass) reuse and extend another class's (the superclass) attributes and methods, modeling an 'is-a' relationship (a Dog is-a Animal) — the subclass automatically gets everything the superclass defines and can override an inherited method's implementation with its own. Polymorphism means the same method call, invoked through a reference of the superclass type, can execute different actual behavior depending on the real runtime type of the object — achieved through dynamic dispatch, where the specific overridden method that runs is determined at runtime based on the object's actual class, not the reference variable's declared type, letting generic code correctly invoke each subclass's specialized behavior without knowing which subclass it's dealing with. Method overriding (a new implementation of an inherited method, same signature, in a subclass) is often confused with method overloading (multiple methods with the same name but different parameter lists within one class) — overriding is resolved at runtime based on actual object type (enabling polymorphism), while overloading is resolved at compile time based on argument types, and the two solve entirely different problems. An abstract class (can't be instantiated directly, may leave some methods unimplemented for subclasses) and an interface (typically a pure contract of method signatures with no implementation) both let code be written against a shared contract rather than a specific concrete class, which is what makes polymorphism useful in practice. The Liskov Substitution Principle captures the deeper correctness requirement inheritance should satisfy: a subclass should be usable anywhere its superclass is expected without breaking the correctness of the code using it, a stronger, behavioral requirement than merely sharing method signatures.","learning_outcomes":["Given a class hierarchy, identify which attributes and methods a subclass inherits, and identify a method it overrides with its own implementation.","Given a superclass reference pointing to different subclass objects at runtime, predict which overridden method implementation executes for each, explaining dynamic dispatch.","Distinguish method overriding from method overloading by identifying whether resolution happens at compile time or runtime, given a specific code example of each."],"common_mistakes":["Confusing overriding with overloading because of the similar names — overriding replaces inherited behavior for polymorphism (runtime resolution), overloading defines multiple same-named methods with different parameters (compile-time resolution).","Assuming the reference variable's declared type determines which overridden method runs — it's the object's actual runtime type that determines this, not the variable's type.","Assuming shared method signatures alone guarantee safe substitution — the Liskov Substitution Principle requires the subclass to also preserve the superclass's expected behavior, not just its signatures.","Treating an abstract class and an interface as interchangeable — an abstract class can share implemented code and state (a class extends only one), while an interface traditionally defines only a contract (a class can implement many)."],"worked_example":{"setup":"Class Animal has makeSound() printing 'Some generic sound'. Subclasses Dog and Cat override it to print 'Woof' and 'Meow'. An Animal[] array holds [new Dog(), new Cat(), new Animal()]. Trace each element's makeSound() call.","steps":["The array is declared Animal[], but each element's actual runtime type is what was used to create it: Dog, Cat, and Animal respectively.","First element: reference declared as Animal, but the actual object is a Dog, so dynamic dispatch invokes Dog's makeSound(), printing 'Woof'.","Second element: actual object is a Cat, so Cat's makeSound() runs, printing 'Meow'.","Third element: actual object is a plain Animal (no override to dispatch to), so Animal's own makeSound() runs, printing 'Some generic sound'."],"result":"The same line of code produces three different outputs depending purely on each element's actual runtime type — code written generically against the Animal type correctly invokes each object's specific behavior without an explicit check for which subclass it's dealing with."}}
{"title":"SOLID Principles","summary":"SOLID is an acronym for five object-oriented design principles intended to make software more maintainable, flexible, and testable. Single Responsibility Principle (SRP) states a class should have only one reason to change — one responsibility — since a class doing multiple unrelated things means a change to one risks breaking unrelated functionality bundled into the same class. Open/Closed Principle (OCP) states software entities should be open for extension but closed for modification — new behavior should be addable by adding new code (like a new subclass) rather than editing existing, already-tested code, commonly achieved through an abstraction that existing code depends on, with new behavior added as new implementations. Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP) states subtypes must be substitutable for their base types without altering the correctness of the program — a subclass should honor its superclass's behavioral contract, not just its method signatures. Interface Segregation Principle (ISP) states clients shouldn't be forced to depend on methods they don't use — favoring several small, specific interfaces over one large general-purpose interface, since a class implementing a bloated interface is forced to stub out irrelevant methods, and any change to that interface can force unrelated implementers to change too. Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP) states high-level modules shouldn't depend directly on low-level modules — both should depend on abstractions, and abstractions shouldn't depend on implementation details — which is what lets a high-level policy remain unaffected when a low-level implementation detail changes, as long as the abstraction's contract stays the same.","learning_outcomes":["Given a code smell or design problem, identify which SOLID principle it violates.","State the precise definition of each of the five SOLID principles and explain, in one sentence each, why violating it causes a concrete maintenance problem.","Given a class implementing a large, general-purpose interface with several irrelevant methods, redesign it to comply with the Interface Segregation Principle."],"common_mistakes":["Confusing 'one reason to change' (SRP) with 'one method' — SRP is about cohesive responsibility, not literal method count.","Assuming Open/Closed means a class can never be modified at all — it means new behavior should be addable without modifying already-working code; fixing an actual bug is a separate concern.","Treating Liskov Substitution as satisfied simply because a subclass compiles and overrides the right methods — LSP is a behavioral requirement, not just a signature-matching one.","Assuming Dependency Inversion means simply using interfaces everywhere — DIP specifically addresses high-level modules depending directly on low-level details; adding an interface with no such dependency-direction problem just adds unnecessary indirection."],"worked_example":{"setup":"A high-level OrderProcessor class directly creates and calls a concrete MySQLDatabase class to save orders. Identify the violated principle and redesign it to comply.","steps":["Identify the violation: OrderProcessor (high-level) depends directly on MySQLDatabase (low-level, concrete) — this violates the Dependency Inversion Principle.","Introduce an abstraction: a Database interface declaring the methods OrderProcessor needs, like save(order), without specifying implementation.","Have MySQLDatabase implement the Database interface, providing the MySQL-specific implementation of save(order).","Change OrderProcessor to depend on the Database interface, receiving a specific implementation from outside rather than creating it itself."],"result":"OrderProcessor now depends only on the Database abstraction — if the system later switches to PostgreSQL, a new PostgresDatabase class implementing the same interface requires zero changes to OrderProcessor, since it never depended on which specific database was used."}}
{"title":"Design Patterns (Gang of Four)","summary":"The Gang of Four (GoF) design patterns — named for the four authors of the 1994 book 'Design Patterns: Elements of Reusable Object-Oriented Software' — are 23 named, reusable solutions to recurring object-oriented design problems, organized into three categories: creational (object creation), structural (how classes and objects compose into larger structures), and behavioral (how objects interact and distribute responsibility). Among the creational patterns, Singleton ensures a class has exactly one instance with a single global access point (useful for shared resources like a configuration manager, though often criticized today for introducing hidden global state that complicates testing), while Factory Method defines an interface for creating an object but lets subclasses decide which concrete class to actually instantiate, decoupling code that needs an object from code that knows how to construct it. Among the structural patterns, Adapter converts one class's interface into another interface client code expects, letting incompatible classes work together without modifying either (like wrapping a legacy class's method names to match a new expected interface), while Decorator attaches additional responsibilities to an object dynamically by wrapping it in another object sharing the same interface, a flexible alternative to subclassing for extending behavior. Among the behavioral patterns, Observer defines a one-to-many dependency where observer objects are automatically notified whenever a subject's state changes (the foundation of most event-handling and reactive-UI systems), while Strategy defines a family of interchangeable algorithms behind a common interface, letting the algorithm used be selected or swapped at runtime without changing the code that uses it. Each pattern exists because a specific recurring design problem kept getting solved in ad hoc, inconsistent ways, and naming and standardizing the solution is what makes design patterns a shared vocabulary experienced developers use to communicate design intent quickly and precisely.","learning_outcomes":["Given a design problem (needing exactly one shared instance, making incompatible interfaces work together, swapping an algorithm at runtime), identify which of the six covered GoF patterns fits.","Explain the distinction between the three GoF pattern categories (creational, structural, behavioral) and correctly classify a named pattern into its category.","Given a code example using one of the six covered patterns, identify its key structural elements and explain how they interact."],"common_mistakes":["Reaching for Singleton whenever 'only one instance is needed' without considering its downsides — it introduces global state that can make unit testing harder and hide dependencies that would otherwise be explicit.","Confusing Adapter with Decorator — Adapter's purpose is making an incompatible interface match what's expected (translation), while Decorator's purpose is adding new behavior to an object with an already-compatible interface (extension).","Assuming a pattern must be applied 'by the book' with the exact class names from the original catalog — patterns are conceptual solutions adapted to a language's idioms and a codebase's needs, not a rigid template.","Confusing Strategy with simply having multiple methods on one class — Strategy specifically encapsulates each algorithm as an interchangeable, independently swappable implementation behind a shared interface."],"worked_example":{"setup":"An e-commerce checkout needs to support paying by CreditCard or PayPal, with new payment methods addable later without modifying checkout code. Show how the Strategy pattern structures this.","steps":["Define a common interface, PaymentStrategy, with a single method pay(amount).","Implement CreditCardPayment and PayPalPayment as separate classes, each implementing pay(amount) with its own specific logic.","The Checkout class holds a reference to a PaymentStrategy (not a specific concrete class), calling strategy.pay(amount) without needing to know which method is actually used underneath.","At runtime, the specific strategy object is set into Checkout based on the user's selection, and Checkout's own code never branches on payment type internally."],"result":"Adding a new payment method later requires only creating a new class implementing PaymentStrategy — Checkout's code requires zero changes, since it only depends on the PaymentStrategy interface, illustrating Strategy's core payoff: swapping the algorithm without touching the code that uses it."}}
{"title":"Distributed Systems Fundamentals","summary":"A distributed system is a collection of independent computers (nodes) that communicate and coordinate only by passing messages over a network, presenting themselves as a single coherent system, without shared memory or a shared physical clock between nodes — this is fundamentally different from a single machine, and that difference is the source of nearly every hard problem in distributed systems. The central challenge is partial failure: in a single machine, catastrophic failure typically fails the whole thing together, but in a distributed system one node can fail while others continue normally, and critically, a requesting node often cannot distinguish 'the remote node crashed' from 'it's just slow' from 'the network dropped my message' — these look identical (silence) from the requester's side, which is why distributed systems must be explicitly designed to handle uncertainty about what actually happened. The 'Fallacies of Distributed Computing' is a well-known list of assumptions that seem intuitively true but are false and repeatedly trip up designs: the network is reliable, latency is zero, bandwidth is infinite, the network is secure, topology doesn't change, there is one administrator, transport cost is zero, and the network is homogeneous — every one is false in real systems, and designing as if any were true produces systems that work in testing but fail in production. Because there's no shared physical clock across nodes, determining the actual order two events happened in is itself hard — clocks on different machines drift and can't be perfectly synchronized, which is why distributed systems often rely on logical clocks (like Lamport timestamps, establishing a consistent 'happened-before' ordering based on message passing rather than physical time). Despite this complexity, distributed systems are built anyway because a single machine has fundamental limits on scalability and fault tolerance (a single point of failure) that distributing work across multiple independent machines is often the only way to meet real-world availability and scale requirements.","learning_outcomes":["Explain why partial failure is the defining challenge of distributed systems, and why a node often cannot distinguish a crashed remote node from a slow one or a lost message.","List at least four of the 'Fallacies of Distributed Computing' and explain why each false assumption can cause a system to fail in production despite working in testing.","Explain why distributed systems rely on logical clocks like Lamport timestamps rather than physical wall-clock time to determine event ordering across machines."],"common_mistakes":["Assuming a distributed system's components fail together like a single machine's — the defining feature is partial failure: some nodes keep working while others fail, slow down, or become unreachable, independently.","Designing as if network calls always succeed quickly and reliably — this produces code that works in local testing (loopback, effectively instantaneous) but fails unpredictably in real deployment.","Assuming timestamps from different machines' physical clocks can be reliably compared to determine event order — clock drift between unsynchronized machines means matching timestamps don't necessarily represent the same actual instant.","Treating 'the request timed out' as equivalent to 'the request definitely failed' — a timeout only means no response arrived in time; the operation may have actually succeeded with a lost or delayed response."],"worked_example":{"setup":"On Machine A, event A1 occurs, then A1 sends a message to Machine B. On Machine B, event B1 occurs independently before the message arrives, then the message arrives triggering B2. Assign Lamport timestamps (both clocks start at 0; local events increment by 1; receiving a message sets clock to max(local, message)+1).","steps":["Event A1 occurs: A's clock increments 0→1, so A1 gets timestamp 1.","A1 sends a message to B, tagged with A's current clock value (1).","Event B1 occurs on B independently, before the message arrives: B's clock increments 0→1, so B1 gets timestamp 1.","The message (tagged 1) arrives at B, triggering B2: new_clock = max(1,1)+1 = 2, so B2 gets timestamp 2."],"result":"A1 and B1 both get timestamp 1 (correctly reflecting they're causally independent — a tie is fine), while B2 gets timestamp 2, correctly placed after both since B2 causally depends on A1's message — exactly what Lamport timestamps guarantee: causally related events get correctly ordered timestamps."}}
{"title":"Consensus Algorithms (Paxos/Raft)","summary":"Consensus is the problem of getting distributed nodes to agree on a single value despite some nodes potentially failing or messages being delayed — this underlies replicated state machines (keeping multiple data copies consistent by agreeing on the order of operations applied to each) and is foundational to reliable distributed databases and coordination services. Consensus is provably hard: the FLP impossibility result (Fischer, Lynch, and Paterson, 1985) proved that in a purely asynchronous system, no deterministic consensus algorithm can guarantee both correctness and termination if even a single process might crash — an adversarial scheduling of messages could in the worst case force the algorithm to wait forever rather than risk an unsafe decision; real algorithms work in practice anyway because real networks aren't adversarially asynchronous in the way FLP's proof requires, and practical algorithms accept the (rare, in practice) risk of not terminating in a pathological worst case rather than sacrificing correctness. Paxos, the original widely-studied consensus algorithm, works through proposers (suggesting a value), acceptors (voting on proposals), and learners (finding out the agreed value), proceeding in a 'prepare' phase where a proposer asks a majority of acceptors to promise not to accept any earlier-numbered proposal, and an 'accept' phase where, having received that majority promise, the proposer asks the same majority to actually accept a specific value — requiring a majority, not all, acceptors is what lets Paxos keep making progress even if a minority of nodes are down. Raft was designed specifically to be more understandable than Paxos while solving the same problem, structuring consensus around a single elected leader at any given time (elected via randomized-timeout-based elections) who handles all client requests and replicates a log of operations to followers in order, with a new election triggered automatically if followers stop hearing from the leader within a timeout. Both algorithms fundamentally rely on requiring agreement from a majority of nodes, rather than all nodes, for any decision, which is what lets the system keep operating correctly even when a minority of nodes have failed, as long as a majority remains reachable.","learning_outcomes":["Explain what problem consensus solves and why it's needed for building a replicated state machine or distributed database.","Explain, at a high level, what the FLP impossibility result actually says (and doesn't say) about consensus in asynchronous systems, and why algorithms like Paxos and Raft are used successfully despite it.","Compare Paxos and Raft's high-level structure, and explain why Raft was designed as an alternative to Paxos."],"common_mistakes":["Misinterpreting FLP as 'consensus is impossible' — it specifically shows guaranteed termination cannot be combined with guaranteed correctness in the worst-case asynchronous model with even one crash failure; real algorithms achieve consensus in virtually all real executions.","Assuming a consensus algorithm requires agreement from every single node — both Paxos and Raft require only a majority, which is precisely what lets the system keep making progress despite a minority being down.","Assuming Raft is less capable than Paxos because it's described as 'simpler' — Raft solves the same problem with equivalent guarantees; its design goal was understandability, not reduced capability.","Assuming a Paxos proposer can propose whatever value it wants in the accept phase regardless of the prepare phase — a correct implementation must account for any already-accepted value acceptors reported, a common source of incorrect implementations."],"worked_example":{"setup":"A 5-node cluster uses majority-based consensus. Nodes 2 and 4 are down; Nodes 1, 3, and 5 are up. Determine whether the cluster can still reach consensus.","steps":["Total nodes = 5; majority requires more than half, so at least 3 nodes.","Reachable nodes: 1, 3, and 5 — exactly 3, meeting the majority threshold.","A proposer/leader among reachable nodes can still get a majority (3) to agree, satisfying quorum, even with 2 of 5 nodes unreachable.","If a third node also went down, only 2 reachable nodes would remain, below the majority threshold of 3, and the cluster could not reach new decisions until enough nodes recover."],"result":"With 3 of 5 nodes reachable, the cluster can still reach consensus, demonstrating why majority quorums (not requiring all nodes) let distributed consensus tolerate failures — but this cluster could tolerate at most 2 simultaneous failures (5−3=2) before losing the ability to decide."}}
{"title":"CAP Theorem & Consistency Models","summary":"The CAP theorem, formally proven by Seth Gilbert and Nancy Lynch in 2002 (building on a conjecture by Eric Brewer), states a distributed data store can provide at most two of three properties simultaneously: Consistency (every read receives the most recent write or an error — all nodes see the same data at the same time), Availability (every request receives a non-error response, without guaranteeing it's the most recent write), and Partition tolerance (the system continues operating despite network partitions, where messages between some nodes are dropped or delayed). Because network partitions are a physical reality that can and do happen in any real distributed system, partition tolerance isn't really an optional design choice — the meaningful choice CAP actually forces is between consistency and availability specifically during an actual partition: a CP system refuses to respond on the minority side of a partition rather than risk stale or conflicting data, while an AP system keeps responding on both sides, accepting that different sides might temporarily disagree. A common misunderstanding is applying CAP's trade-off to a system's entire normal operation — CAP's guarantee specifically concerns behavior during an actual partition; when no partition is occurring, a well-designed system can and should provide both consistency and availability, so CAP isn't a permanent, always-active trade-off, only one that manifests when a partition actually happens. Consistency itself isn't binary but a spectrum: strong (linearizable) consistency makes a system behave as if there were only a single copy of the data, with every operation appearing to take effect instantaneously at some point between its start and completion; eventual consistency only guarantees replicas converge if writes stop; and causal consistency sits between the two, guaranteeing causally related operations (like a comment appearing after the post it replies to) are seen in the same order by everyone, while unrelated operations may be seen in different orders by different observers. PACELC, a refinement of CAP proposed by Daniel Abadi, extends the framework into two cases: if a Partition occurs, the trade-off is between Availability and Consistency, exactly as CAP describes (the 'PAC' part); but Else, even during entirely normal operation with no partition at all, a system still has to trade Latency for Consistency (the 'ELC' part), since achieving strong consistency across replicas generally requires more coordination, and therefore more latency, than accepting weaker consistency would.","learning_outcomes":["State the CAP theorem's three properties precisely and explain why partition tolerance is effectively non-negotiable for real distributed systems, making the actual choice CP versus AP during a partition.","Explain the common misunderstanding of CAP as a constant, always-active trade-off, and clarify what CAP actually guarantees only during an active network partition.","Explain PACELC's extension of CAP, including what trade-off it identifies for normal operation that CAP itself doesn't address."],"common_mistakes":["Believing CAP means a system must always sacrifice one of the three properties at all times — the trade-off specifically applies during an actual partition; outside of one, a well-designed system can provide both consistency and availability.","Treating partition tolerance as optional — network partitions are a physical reality, so in practice the meaningful choice is between consistency and availability when a partition occurs, not whether to tolerate partitions at all.","Assuming 'availability' in the CAP sense means everyday uptime — it specifically means every request receives a non-error response, even if that response might not reflect the most recent write.","Assuming CAP fully describes the trade-off space — PACELC points out that even with no partition, systems still trade latency against consistency during entirely normal operation."],"worked_example":{"setup":"A key-value store has replicas in Data Center East and West, both holding X=100. A network partition severs East-West communication. A client connected to West sends: set X=200. Trace a CP system versus an AP system.","steps":["CP system: West cannot confirm the write with East (or reach a majority quorum, since 2 replicas alone can't form one without agreement), so West refuses the write, returning an error rather than risking divergence.","AP system: West accepts the write locally despite being unable to reach East, updating its own copy to X=200 and returning success — West remains available.","Meanwhile East, unaware of the write, still has X=100; a client reading from East during the partition would see the stale value 100.","Once the partition heals, the AP system must reconcile: East and West now disagree (100 vs 200) and need a conflict-resolution mechanism to converge."],"result":"The CP system sacrificed availability (rejecting the write) to avoid replica disagreement, while the AP system sacrificed consistency (accepting the write, creating temporary disagreement) to keep accepting requests — the same partition, handled in opposite ways depending on which property the system prioritizes."}}
